WEBVTT

00:00.917 --> 00:04.537
♪ It seems today that all you see ♪

00:04.538 --> 00:07.972
♪ Is violence in movies and sex on TV ♪

00:07.973 --> 00:11.668
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.669 --> 00:14.814
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.815 --> 00:17.511
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:17.512 --> 00:21.305
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.306 --> 00:23.043
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.044 --> 00:24.729
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.730 --> 00:29.729
♪ He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:38.943 --> 00:41.043
Send in Stewie, please.

00:41.078 --> 00:42.444
Oh, I can go in now?

00:42.479 --> 00:45.247
<i>Oh, fantastic. He's ready. Marvelous.</i>

00:45.282 --> 00:46.315
Thank you, Barbara.

00:46.350 --> 00:47.650
I hope you get those Adele tickets.

00:47.651 --> 00:49.017
You deserve them.

00:49.053 --> 00:50.352
She won't get them. No way.

00:50.387 --> 00:52.087
She needed to get them,
like, a month ago.

00:52.122 --> 00:53.956
So, um, is that me over there?

00:53.991 --> 00:56.725
- If you like.
- Here we go.

01:00.998 --> 01:03.232
I saw the kid who has cancer leave.

01:03.267 --> 01:04.766
That must've been fun.

01:04.802 --> 01:07.436
I don't know why his parents are
still bringing him to school.

01:07.471 --> 01:09.104
It's uncomfortable for everyone.

01:09.139 --> 01:12.107
I've never seen him
when he's not just thrown up.

01:13.644 --> 01:15.410
Charming. This is charming.

01:15.446 --> 01:18.413
It reminds me of the therapist's
office Bethenny Frankel goes to

01:18.449 --> 01:21.116
<i>on The Real Housewives of New York City.</i>

01:21.151 --> 01:22.484
I hate her.

01:22.519 --> 01:24.386
She looks like a wooden doll you'd find

01:24.421 --> 01:26.722
in an Eastern European toy shop.

01:26.757 --> 01:29.057
Oh, don't act
like you don't know who she is.

01:29.093 --> 01:30.659
Please, that doesn't impress me.

01:30.694 --> 01:31.793
We live in the world.

01:31.829 --> 01:33.662
We all know who Bethenny Frankel is,

01:33.697 --> 01:35.530
whether we like it or not.

01:35.566 --> 01:38.200
You know, I actually don't mind her.

01:38.235 --> 01:40.202
And she's built quite a business
for herself.

01:40.237 --> 01:42.070
You have to hand it to her.

01:42.106 --> 01:45.474
I don't mean to name-drop,
but I kind of know Andy Cohen.

01:45.509 --> 01:46.942
I guess I'm his type.

01:46.977 --> 01:48.477
Which, of course, is very flattering.

01:48.512 --> 01:50.212
He's outrageously successful.

01:50.247 --> 01:53.248
<i>He originally wanted me
for The Rachel Zoe Project,</i>

01:53.284 --> 01:55.217
but I said no, so they went with Brad.

01:55.252 --> 01:57.953
<i>And now look... that could've
been me on Fashion Police</i>

01:57.988 --> 02:00.155
with Melissa Rivers 14 times a year.

02:00.190 --> 02:02.891
Ah, well, hindsight.

02:02.926 --> 02:05.027
I see the tissue box is empty.

02:05.062 --> 02:07.296
Somebody was going through those like...

02:07.331 --> 02:09.431
well, tissues.

02:09.466 --> 02:12.167
Mm, I hope he didn't get cancer
all over this seat.

02:12.202 --> 02:13.802
Ugh. Yuck.

02:13.837 --> 02:15.070
Bad luck.

02:15.105 --> 02:17.406
Although I did hear
Ryan Reynolds visited him.

02:17.441 --> 02:18.975
<i>Something to do with Deadpool, I guess.</i>

02:18.976 --> 02:20.609
So it's not all horrible.

02:20.644 --> 02:22.944
If they don't give us a day off
when he dies,

02:22.980 --> 02:24.846
I'm gonna be so pissed.

02:24.882 --> 02:28.016
Oh, look, you got
your Dunkin' Donuts coffee.

02:28.052 --> 02:30.519
I don't know why those cups
always depress me.

02:30.554 --> 02:32.354
It's-it's like you've already given up

02:32.389 --> 02:33.989
before the day's even started.

02:34.024 --> 02:36.458
You might as well tattoo
"Can't afford Starbucks"

02:36.493 --> 02:37.959
on your forehead.

02:37.995 --> 02:39.361
What do I care, though?

02:39.396 --> 02:41.630
None of my business.

02:43.434 --> 02:45.368
I haven't heard of any
of the schools you went to,

02:45.369 --> 02:46.601
but I'm sure they were great.

02:46.637 --> 02:48.136
I mean, you're here, right?

02:48.172 --> 02:50.305
<i>And why are you here, Stewie?</i>

02:50.341 --> 02:51.673
Uh, that was lame.

02:51.709 --> 02:54.276
You seem to have
a lot of strong opinions.

02:54.311 --> 02:57.079
I do. Mostly because
I come across a lot of dicks.

02:57.114 --> 02:59.214
Or I guess I should say "wankers."

02:59.249 --> 03:00.949
We do like that word, hmm?

03:00.984 --> 03:02.818
Or "tosser," "git," "prat,"

03:02.853 --> 03:04.486
"sod," "chuffer."

03:04.521 --> 03:06.888
Take your pick, eh, Dr. Pritchfield?

03:06.924 --> 03:08.623
- "We"?
- Yes, "we."

03:08.659 --> 03:10.125
I'm not following.

03:10.160 --> 03:11.960
- The accent.
- The accent?

03:11.995 --> 03:13.762
You speak with a British accent.

03:13.797 --> 03:15.297
Yes, I'm aware.

03:15.332 --> 03:17.899
Well, I've often been told
that I have a British accent.

03:17.935 --> 03:19.267
Oh?

03:19.303 --> 03:21.941
That's-that's all you're going to say?
"Oh"?

03:21.942 --> 03:24.173
What is it you'd like me to say, Stewie?

03:24.208 --> 03:26.174
That I have a (BLEEP) British accent.

03:26.210 --> 03:28.610
I apologize, but I can't hear it.

03:28.645 --> 03:30.246
You mean to tell me
you don't hear me speaking

03:30.247 --> 03:32.781
- with a British accent right now?
- No.

03:32.816 --> 03:35.450
That's odd, because everybody else does.

03:35.486 --> 03:38.253
Have you spent much time
in Britain, then?

03:38.288 --> 03:39.755
No, I haven't.

03:39.790 --> 03:41.423
- Excuse me?
- I said no!

03:41.458 --> 03:44.326
But you're the absolute only
person who hasn't noticed it.

03:44.361 --> 03:46.628
Really? Everyone hears this?

03:46.663 --> 03:48.430
Well, just the people
who can understand me,

03:48.465 --> 03:49.965
which you appear to be one of.

03:50.000 --> 03:51.700
But that's too complicated to go into.

03:51.735 --> 03:53.034
I'm not even sure I get it.

03:53.070 --> 03:55.570
The point is,
Stewie has a British accent.

03:55.606 --> 03:56.838
That's, like, a known thing.

03:56.874 --> 03:58.106
Ask anyone.

03:58.142 --> 04:00.008
I'm afraid I don't hear it.

04:00.043 --> 04:01.610
Yeah, you've said.

04:01.645 --> 04:03.512
It sounds like it's very important

04:03.547 --> 04:05.580
what other people think of you.

04:05.616 --> 04:07.282
Oh, is-is that what it sounds like?

04:07.317 --> 04:09.418
To me, it does, yes.

04:09.453 --> 04:10.719
To you it does. I see, I see.

04:10.754 --> 04:13.455
So after five minutes, you think
you know me, is that it?

04:13.490 --> 04:15.257
A little, perhaps.

04:15.292 --> 04:17.192
I am trained, Stewie, to observe things

04:17.227 --> 04:18.527
that give me an insight.

04:18.562 --> 04:21.496
And, yes, I do feel I know you a bit.

04:21.532 --> 04:23.632
You do feel you know me a bit.
Mm-hmm. Okay.

04:23.667 --> 04:25.901
All right, all right.
Well, that's-that's fair.

04:26.970 --> 04:28.370
Might I?

04:28.405 --> 04:31.273
I'm not sure how this is going to help.

04:31.341 --> 04:32.474
Indulge me.

04:34.011 --> 04:36.144
Thank you.

04:36.180 --> 04:39.381
I see you're on vacation
in Rio de Janeiro in this photo

04:39.416 --> 04:40.849
with your partner, I'm guessing.

04:40.884 --> 04:42.284
Or is he your husband?

04:42.319 --> 04:44.686
Yes, probably wanted
to make it official, didn't you?

04:44.721 --> 04:46.355
Never thought you'd see it
in your lifetime.

04:46.356 --> 04:48.023
Blah, blah, blah, blah.

04:48.058 --> 04:49.791
At a quick glance,
I'd say there's roughly

04:49.827 --> 04:52.494
a 26-year age difference
between the two of you.

04:52.529 --> 04:54.563
Not quite large enough to raise eyebrows

04:54.598 --> 04:56.832
while still giving you
a younger mind to shape.

04:56.867 --> 04:58.834
<i>"Oh, you mean you haven't seen
All About Eve,</i>

04:58.869 --> 05:01.303
<i>Gypsy, Valley of the Dolls?" Et cetera.</i>

05:01.338 --> 05:04.706
And a younger body
to make you feel more virile.

05:04.741 --> 05:06.708
Taking into consideration the math,

05:06.743 --> 05:08.643
he was probably born at a time

05:08.679 --> 05:10.912
when the most popular name was...

05:10.948 --> 05:13.081
Michael.

05:13.116 --> 05:15.717
From the dog-eared pages
of that volume of Shakespeare,

05:15.752 --> 05:20.055
I imagine you wooed Michael with
a sonnet or monologue each day.

05:20.090 --> 05:22.591
And no one had ever done
anything so romantic

05:22.626 --> 05:24.860
as reciting Shakespeare
for Michael before.

05:24.895 --> 05:26.528
And he was smitten.

05:26.563 --> 05:28.263
While not as physically attracted to you

05:28.298 --> 05:30.031
as you are to him, that's okay...

05:30.067 --> 05:32.701
You each bring something
important to the relationship.

05:32.736 --> 05:35.036
Isn't that what you believe,
Dr. Pritchfield?

05:36.039 --> 05:37.472
I see a prescription

05:37.508 --> 05:39.341
for heart medication on that table.

05:39.376 --> 05:41.076
My guess is you keep that at the office.

05:41.111 --> 05:43.211
You don't want to worry Michael,
you say to yourself,

05:43.247 --> 05:45.881
even though the truth is
it embarrasses you.

05:45.916 --> 05:47.549
Your age, your mortality.

05:47.584 --> 05:49.417
Best to keep appearing
as vital as possible.

05:49.453 --> 05:51.152
And no need to have reminders at home

05:51.188 --> 05:53.922
of what's just around
the corner, is there?

05:53.957 --> 05:55.457
And your office is your haven,

05:55.492 --> 05:57.759
since Michael doesn't work
and is often at home,

05:57.794 --> 05:59.828
perhaps as a result
of a low-level depression

05:59.863 --> 06:01.763
that you don't really want
to get into with him.

06:01.798 --> 06:03.765
Oh, he always planned to have a career,

06:03.800 --> 06:05.834
but he could never settle
on exactly what it was

06:05.869 --> 06:07.202
he wanted to do.

06:07.237 --> 06:08.804
And at first
you liked having him at home

06:08.805 --> 06:11.039
to take care of the house
and plan trips.

06:11.074 --> 06:12.741
So that's just what happened.

06:12.776 --> 06:16.077
"And that's a job, too,"
he reminds you over the years.

06:16.113 --> 06:18.713
Michael likes to travel,
and he prefers the finer things.

06:18.749 --> 06:20.669
And you'd like to give them
to him, wouldn't you?

06:20.684 --> 06:23.485
But it's a bit difficult
on a child psychologist's salary

06:23.520 --> 06:25.887
at a Rhode Island preschool,
I'd imagine.

06:25.923 --> 06:27.390
Fortunately, you don't have children,

06:27.391 --> 06:29.758
so you do have some disposable income.

06:29.793 --> 06:31.960
<i>Oh, you have talked about it
with other couples,</i>

06:31.995 --> 06:33.962
just for show, probably at dinner

06:33.997 --> 06:35.397
with younger friends of Michael's

06:35.432 --> 06:36.631
who are starting a family,

06:36.667 --> 06:38.433
you know,
to be part of the conversation,

06:38.468 --> 06:39.634
to feel included.

06:39.670 --> 06:41.037
But you said you preferred your trips

06:41.038 --> 06:43.038
and your rescue dog
named after a character

06:43.073 --> 06:44.806
most likely from Dickens.

06:44.841 --> 06:46.775
Ah, how accomplished and affluent

06:46.810 --> 06:48.243
you both look in this photo.

06:48.278 --> 06:51.046
Just the image Michael
is so desperate to project.

06:51.081 --> 06:53.315
Let's look a little closer, shall we?

06:53.350 --> 06:55.917
Now, I see you're both wearing
Ralph Lauren Purple Label

06:55.953 --> 06:59.321
dress shirts
that retail starting at $495.

06:59.356 --> 07:01.289
But from the disfigured
button holes on one

07:01.325 --> 07:03.058
and the small discoloration
on the other,

07:03.093 --> 07:05.460
I can see you bought them
at the outlet in Providence.

07:05.495 --> 07:06.861
Probably third markdown,

07:06.897 --> 07:09.064
in which case, $49, give or take.

07:09.099 --> 07:11.066
Since you also have light jackets on,

07:11.101 --> 07:13.268
I can see that you went there
during summer vacation,

07:13.303 --> 07:15.403
which is, in fact, winter in Rio,

07:15.439 --> 07:17.606
outside of the high tourist season.

07:17.641 --> 07:19.608
And I see that you're
on the rooftop pool deck

07:19.643 --> 07:21.610
at the Fasano Hotel in Ipanema,

07:21.645 --> 07:24.546
the most exclusive hotel
in all of Brazil.

07:24.581 --> 07:26.548
Michael would've been dying
to stay there.

07:26.583 --> 07:28.650
<i>Had a friend that went
with his older boyfriend,</i>

07:28.685 --> 07:30.785
who makes a lot of money
in, most likely, banking,

07:30.821 --> 07:32.120
much to your chagrin.

07:32.155 --> 07:35.123
But even in August,
it's almost $1,000 a night.

07:35.158 --> 07:37.792
You tell him people are starving
in the streets in Brazil.

07:37.828 --> 07:39.995
How do you justify paying those prices?

07:40.030 --> 07:42.330
When the truth is,
you simply can't afford it.

07:42.366 --> 07:44.265
It's for another class of gay people.

07:44.301 --> 07:45.467
The window that Michael

07:45.502 --> 07:47.402
is always desperately peering through

07:47.437 --> 07:49.004
and sadly on the other side of.

07:49.039 --> 07:50.472
But you do your best.

07:50.507 --> 07:52.941
You don't go on Airbnb,
because you don't trust it,

07:52.976 --> 07:54.577
even though Michael has stories
of friends

07:54.578 --> 07:56.611
who have found the most fabulous places.

07:56.647 --> 07:57.912
You like a hotel.

07:57.948 --> 07:59.948
Besides, it's more romantic,
you tell him.

07:59.983 --> 08:02.317
But when you take him to the
place you're actually staying,

08:02.352 --> 08:05.387
the one you found on TripAdvisor
that was rated number 27

08:05.422 --> 08:06.888
of all the hotels in Rio

08:06.923 --> 08:10.325
and was having a special rate
of 295 U.S. dollars a night...

08:10.360 --> 08:12.293
Which is still not cheap,
you remind him,

08:12.329 --> 08:14.929
most people never get to visit
half the places he's seen,

08:14.965 --> 08:17.799
you tell him... you can't help
but feel like a bit of a failure

08:17.834 --> 08:19.801
as you see the look
of disappointment on his face

08:19.836 --> 08:21.503
as he enters the room.

08:21.538 --> 08:23.171
So you decide to go to the Fasano Hotel

08:23.206 --> 08:25.173
for dinner and drinks. No, just drinks,

08:25.208 --> 08:27.642
once you've seen
the restaurant prices online.

08:27.678 --> 08:29.844
And once there, you can see
how Michael begins looking

08:29.880 --> 08:31.813
<i>at all the older men who can afford</i>

08:31.848 --> 08:34.115
to take their boyfriends...
I'm sorry, husbands...

08:34.151 --> 08:36.351
To such luxurious hotels.

08:36.386 --> 08:38.120
And you worry a little,
that you're diminishing

08:38.121 --> 08:40.088
in his eyes with each passing year.

08:40.123 --> 08:42.023
But you tell yourself
you're being ridiculous,

08:42.059 --> 08:44.759
as you see Michael
glancing about the rooftop deck,

08:44.795 --> 08:46.428
looking at all the young men,

08:46.463 --> 08:48.997
men that would be 20 years
younger than even Michael,

08:49.032 --> 08:50.632
men that you're invisible to.

08:50.667 --> 08:52.767
But he isn't yet, not completely,

08:52.803 --> 08:55.937
and you're jealous
and you loathe yourself for it.

08:55.972 --> 08:57.305
But Michael wants a photo,

08:57.340 --> 08:58.740
and he says, "Let's take a selfie."

08:58.775 --> 09:01.209
But you say, "Let's ask someone
to take one of us"

09:01.244 --> 09:04.212
and make some tired joke about
selfies that only you laugh at,

09:04.247 --> 09:06.347
wishing that you could take it
out of the air

09:06.383 --> 09:09.017
as soon as you've said it,
as it's just one more indicator

09:09.052 --> 09:11.720
that you're older than everybody here.

09:11.755 --> 09:14.756
So Michael calls over a young
man wearing a tiny swimsuit

09:14.791 --> 09:17.926
to take the photo and makes a
crude joke that embarrasses you.

09:17.961 --> 09:19.494
But the young man laughs,

09:19.529 --> 09:21.810
and he and Michael share a
moment that you're not part of.

09:21.832 --> 09:23.798
And you feel humiliated and unseen.

09:23.834 --> 09:25.867
Which explains Michael's joyful grin

09:25.902 --> 09:28.203
and your slightly disconnected
half-smile.

09:28.238 --> 09:30.205
And you've posted the photo on Facebook,

09:30.240 --> 09:33.208
and Michael's posted it to
his 86 followers on Instagram...

09:33.243 --> 09:34.843
You don't know how that one works...

09:34.878 --> 09:36.644
To at least give the impression,

09:36.680 --> 09:38.146
in your Ralph Lauren shirts,

09:38.181 --> 09:40.014
holding your $20 cocktails,

09:40.050 --> 09:42.650
that you're both way more
successful than you are.

09:42.686 --> 09:44.185
But it makes you feel a little dirty,

09:44.221 --> 09:47.188
the lengths you have to go to
in order to keep Michael happy.

09:47.224 --> 09:49.457
And every time you glance
at this picture,

09:49.493 --> 09:52.927
you wonder,
"How long before he leaves me?"

09:52.963 --> 09:56.264
See? I guess we both know
each other a bit.

09:58.368 --> 10:01.336
You seem like a very lonely little boy.

10:01.371 --> 10:03.738
Oh, my God, I am!

10:03.774 --> 10:05.573
I'm so lonely!

10:07.711 --> 10:09.010
Oh!

10:09.045 --> 10:12.413
You can see inside my soul!

10:13.450 --> 10:15.950
Oh, God!

10:21.458 --> 10:22.590
Thank you.

10:22.626 --> 10:25.059
Thank you, Barbara.

10:29.132 --> 10:31.432
Are you all right now, Stewie?

10:31.468 --> 10:32.834
Yes, I think.

10:32.869 --> 10:34.369
You're very kind.

10:34.404 --> 10:36.304
She's a gem, that one, you know.

10:36.339 --> 10:37.639
I hope you appreciate her.

10:37.674 --> 10:39.808
Try taking some deep breaths.

10:46.049 --> 10:47.749
I do this sometimes in yoga.

10:49.052 --> 10:50.151
I take yoga.

10:51.721 --> 10:52.921
I have a sick body.

10:56.359 --> 10:58.927
I do... I do feel a bit better.

10:58.962 --> 11:00.929
But still lonely.

11:00.964 --> 11:02.897
I'm sorry. It keeps spilling out.

11:02.933 --> 11:05.533
Oh, God. Oh, God.

11:05.569 --> 11:08.670
It seems like you're keeping
a lot bottled up inside.

11:08.705 --> 11:11.639
I am. Nobody here likes me,
Dr. Pritchfield.

11:11.675 --> 11:13.775
I try to fit in with the other boys

11:13.810 --> 11:15.977
and talk about things
like dirt and shapes,

11:16.012 --> 11:17.445
but they won't play with me.

11:17.480 --> 11:19.113
And I-I don't have any friends,

11:19.149 --> 11:21.416
and I have nobody to eat lunch with.

11:21.451 --> 11:23.652
It's very interesting you'd say
that you have no friends,

11:23.653 --> 11:26.821
Stewie, especially considering
the reason you're here.

11:26.857 --> 11:28.323
Oh. That.

11:28.358 --> 11:29.991
Shall we talk about it?

11:30.026 --> 11:31.726
I don't know
what there is to talk about.

11:31.761 --> 11:34.195
You pushed a classmate down the stairs.

11:34.231 --> 11:36.898
<i>It was an accident.
Haven't you ever seen Showgirls?</i>

11:36.933 --> 11:39.300
Yes, of course I have.
But according to Tyler,

11:39.336 --> 11:40.869
it was no accident.

11:40.904 --> 11:43.171
Well, I guess it's my word
against his, then.

11:43.206 --> 11:45.173
It is creating
a bit of a con-trah-versy.

11:45.208 --> 11:46.975
- Excuse me?
- A con-trah-versy.

11:47.010 --> 11:49.244
- I don't know what that is.
- Con-trah-versy?

11:49.279 --> 11:50.812
"Con-trah-versy"?

11:50.847 --> 11:52.914
Oh! Oh, "con-troversy."

11:52.949 --> 11:55.650
Apologies.
Those of us with British accents

11:55.685 --> 11:57.185
pronounce it "con-trah-versy."

11:57.220 --> 11:58.720
But how would you know that?

11:58.755 --> 11:59.921
Yeah, how would I know that?

11:59.956 --> 12:01.189
Look, Dr. Pritchfield...

12:01.224 --> 12:02.724
"Cee-cil," if I may?

12:02.759 --> 12:04.392
It's pronounced "Cess-il."

12:04.427 --> 12:06.194
- Damn it!
- But, again, how would you know?

12:06.229 --> 12:08.930
- It's not as if you're...
- Okay, okay, we got it.

12:08.965 --> 12:11.199
I only pushed Tyler down
the stairs because I like him

12:11.234 --> 12:12.767
and I'm afraid he won't like me back.

12:12.802 --> 12:15.236
And-and not like him-like him.
I'm not gay.

12:15.272 --> 12:17.839
This whole thing isn't because
I'm gay, so-so calm down.

12:17.874 --> 12:19.908
I can already see you
lickin' your chops.

12:19.943 --> 12:22.977
I'm sure you live
for the coming out sessions.

12:23.013 --> 12:25.580
If anything,
I'm less gay than I used to be.

12:25.615 --> 12:27.916
Not that anybody
at this school would care.

12:27.951 --> 12:30.451
But do I think that
Grant Gustin and I would make

12:30.487 --> 12:32.120
the most adorable Instagram couple?

12:32.155 --> 12:34.088
Yes. Yes, we would.

12:34.124 --> 12:37.125
Grant Gustin plays the Flash on
the CW, if you were wondering.

12:37.160 --> 12:39.227
Because, you know, most people over 70

12:39.262 --> 12:41.462
probably don't know who he is.

12:41.498 --> 12:44.032
Think young Anthony Perkins.

12:44.067 --> 12:45.767
Ah. Oh.

12:45.802 --> 12:47.302
There we go.

12:47.337 --> 12:48.903
Anyway, "fluid" is something I hear

12:48.939 --> 12:50.471
being tossed around a lot now.

12:50.507 --> 12:52.874
But I'm confident in my heterosexuality.

12:52.909 --> 12:55.143
- That's a word, right?
- Yes, of course.

12:55.178 --> 12:57.946
Okay, just sounded strange for a second.

12:57.981 --> 13:01.516
It must be difficult for you,
Stewie, being so intelligent.

13:01.551 --> 13:02.984
It is.

13:03.019 --> 13:05.186
Whew! Just that...

13:05.221 --> 13:07.188
just that acknowledgment.

13:07.223 --> 13:10.091
You know, sometimes
I don't know where I fit in.

13:10.126 --> 13:13.761
I just... I just want
to be like everybody else,

13:13.797 --> 13:16.931
<i>but nobody's interested in the
things that I'm interested in.</i>

13:16.967 --> 13:18.333
They think I'm weird.

13:18.368 --> 13:20.101
And I'm so anxious all the time,

13:20.136 --> 13:23.237
but I... I always put on
a brave face and try to hide it,

13:23.273 --> 13:25.606
and I... I give myself
these excruciating migraines.

13:25.675 --> 13:28.142
I actually have way more hair than this,

13:28.178 --> 13:30.378
but I pull it out.

13:30.413 --> 13:32.647
I'm sorry, now I'm getting
all worked up again.

13:32.682 --> 13:34.716
I'm worse than Bethenny Frankel
on her period.

13:34.751 --> 13:36.584
I don't why I keep bringing her up.

13:36.619 --> 13:38.886
I j... it's just I think
about her a lot.

13:38.955 --> 13:41.689
<i>Well, what are the things
you're interested in, Stewie?</i>

13:41.725 --> 13:44.459
Me? Well, I like to talk
about world domination.

13:44.494 --> 13:47.228
Some people don't think
I talk about it enough anymore,

13:47.263 --> 13:50.064
but mind your own beeswax
is what I say to them.

13:50.100 --> 13:52.200
I also like to talk about musicals.

13:52.235 --> 13:54.502
It's hard to find someone
who can converse

13:54.537 --> 13:56.571
on one of those topics, let alone both.

13:56.606 --> 13:58.873
<i>And I really, really,
really want to see Hamilton,</i>

13:58.908 --> 14:01.409
but by the time
it gets here, I'll be 30.

14:01.444 --> 14:04.312
I think I'm still the only one
in town who's even

14:04.347 --> 14:06.948
heard of it, and it's been out
for over two years.

14:06.983 --> 14:09.317
Which just makes me even more depressed.

14:09.352 --> 14:11.619
How do you live in a place like that?

14:11.654 --> 14:13.488
Quahog. What a pile of garbage.

14:13.523 --> 14:15.356
And I've even memorized
some of the songs,

14:15.392 --> 14:18.292
but I have no one to sing
it for, and I'm really good.

14:18.328 --> 14:20.561
- Do you want to hear it?
- Oh. Um...

14:20.597 --> 14:22.163
Y-You mean now?

14:22.198 --> 14:25.199
<i>I really... I really need you
to hear me sing Hamilton.</i>

14:25.235 --> 14:28.136
<i>I'm so good. Why can't I be on Broadway?</i>

14:28.171 --> 14:29.737
<i>I want to be on Broadway.</i>

14:29.773 --> 14:33.274
I want the world to fall in love
with me eight times a week.

14:33.309 --> 14:35.176
I'd love to hear you sing.

14:35.211 --> 14:37.245
Okay, okay, now I'm nervous.

14:37.280 --> 14:40.448
If it's not good this time, just
remember it was good at home.

14:40.483 --> 14:42.316
Don't look at me while I start.

14:42.352 --> 14:43.551
It's hard.

14:43.586 --> 14:45.620
It's a lot of words very fast.

14:45.655 --> 14:48.456
Like Gilbert and Sullivan,
but for Hispanics.

14:50.627 --> 14:53.661
♪ How does a bastard orphan ♪

14:53.696 --> 14:55.396
♪ Son of a whore ♪

14:55.432 --> 14:57.665
♪ And a Scotsman dropped in the middle ♪

14:57.700 --> 15:00.268
♪ Of a forgotten spot in the Caribbean ♪

15:00.303 --> 15:04.005
♪ By providence impoverished
in squalor grow up to be ♪

15:04.040 --> 15:05.807
♪ A hero and a scholar? ♪

15:05.842 --> 15:08.476
♪ The ten dollar Founding Father
without a father ♪

15:08.511 --> 15:11.479
♪ Got a lot farther
by working a lot harder ♪

15:11.514 --> 15:13.214
♪ By being a lot smarter ♪

15:13.249 --> 15:15.149
♪ By being a self-starter ♪

15:15.185 --> 15:18.920
♪ By 14, they placed him
in charge of a trading charter ♪

15:18.955 --> 15:21.389
♪ And every day while slaves
were being slaughtered ♪

15:21.424 --> 15:23.991
♪ And carted away across the waves ♪

15:24.027 --> 15:25.626
♪ He struggled and kept his guard up ♪

15:25.662 --> 15:28.563
♪ Inside, he was longing
for something to be a part of ♪

15:28.598 --> 15:31.032
♪ The brother was ready to beg, steal ♪

15:31.067 --> 15:32.500
♪ Borrow or barter ♪

15:32.535 --> 15:35.536
♪ Then a hurricane came
and devastation reigned ♪

15:35.572 --> 15:37.738
♪ Our man saw his future drip ♪

15:37.774 --> 15:40.608
♪ Dripping down the drain,
put a pencil to his temple ♪

15:40.643 --> 15:42.343
♪ Connected it to his brain ♪

15:42.378 --> 15:44.078
♪ And he wrote his first refrain ♪

15:44.114 --> 15:46.414
♪ A testament to his pain... ♪

15:53.256 --> 15:54.989
♪ Well, the word got around ♪

15:55.024 --> 15:57.125
♪ And they said,
"This kid is insane, man" ♪

15:57.160 --> 16:00.428
♪ Took up a collection just
to send him to the mainland ♪

16:00.463 --> 16:01.762
♪ Get your education ♪

16:01.798 --> 16:03.464
♪ Don't forget from whence you came ♪

16:03.500 --> 16:05.900
♪ And the world's gonna know your name ♪

16:05.935 --> 16:07.368
♪ What's your name, man? ♪

16:07.403 --> 16:09.570
♪ Alexander Hamilton ♪

16:09.606 --> 16:12.106
♪ My name is Alexander Hamilton ♪

16:12.142 --> 16:15.176
♪ And there's a million things
I haven't done ♪

16:15.211 --> 16:17.879
♪ But just you wait, just you wait. ♪

16:19.249 --> 16:21.883
That's all I've learned so far.

16:21.918 --> 16:23.618
Well done!

16:23.653 --> 16:26.320
You're a very special
little boy, Stewie.

16:26.356 --> 16:28.356
I saw you check your watch.

16:28.391 --> 16:30.458
That was a bit emotional
for you, wasn't it?

16:30.493 --> 16:33.928
But it felt good. Like-like,
I got so much out, you know?

16:33.963 --> 16:37.632
<i>- Would you like a cup of tea?
- I would love a cup of tea.</i>

16:41.237 --> 16:43.304
<i>It's hot already?</i>

16:43.339 --> 16:45.439
Yes, it's an electric kettle.

16:45.475 --> 16:47.275
We don't have that at our house.

16:47.310 --> 16:50.378
My mother boils water
in an old Jiffy Pop tin.

16:50.413 --> 16:52.413
She's just a useless old slut.

16:52.448 --> 16:54.448
Hey, my hiccups are gone!

16:54.484 --> 16:57.518
If you liked Tyler and you
wanted to be friends with him,

16:57.554 --> 17:00.087
then why did you
push him down the stairs?

17:00.123 --> 17:03.357
Oh. I... I don't know.

17:03.393 --> 17:06.294
Can I tell you a story
about another little boy?

17:06.329 --> 17:08.129
Um, okay.

17:08.164 --> 17:10.398
When I was your age, I grew up in London

17:10.433 --> 17:13.467
<i>- when the Blitz had just ended.
- Good God, how old are you?</i>

17:13.503 --> 17:16.204
Our neighborhood was reduced to rubble.

17:19.609 --> 17:23.077
<i>And in the chaos, I searched
desperately for my parents.</i>

17:24.314 --> 17:26.247
Mum. Mum.

17:26.282 --> 17:28.082
Mother. Mother.

17:28.117 --> 17:30.084
Mum. Mummy.

17:30.119 --> 17:32.954
Mummy. Mummy. Mummy.

17:32.989 --> 17:35.423
Mummy. Mum. Mother.

17:35.458 --> 17:37.258
<i>- Don't.
- Don't what?</i>

17:37.293 --> 17:38.793
<i>You know what.</i>

17:38.828 --> 17:40.895
<i>But Mother was nowhere to be found.</i>

17:40.930 --> 17:43.931
<i>And neither was Father.</i>

17:43.967 --> 17:47.368
<i>You see, I was
the sole survivor of my family.</i>

17:47.403 --> 17:49.804
<i>So I was sent to live
with an aunt in Cornwall.</i>

17:49.839 --> 17:53.608
<i>But she had too many mouths
of her own to feed, so...</i>

17:53.643 --> 17:56.944
<i>I was next sent to a distant
cousin in Northumberland.</i>

17:56.980 --> 17:58.713
<i>But she didn't care for children,</i>

17:58.748 --> 18:00.348
<i>and off again I went,</i>

18:00.383 --> 18:02.717
<i>this time to an orphanage
in Gloucestershire.</i>

18:02.752 --> 18:04.151
<i>But they didn't like little boys</i>

18:04.187 --> 18:05.486
<i>from London in Gloucestershire.</i>

18:05.521 --> 18:07.622
<i>Or from Cornwall or Northumberland.</i>

18:07.657 --> 18:10.791
<i>So I pretended I was from
Shropshire, of all places.</i>

18:10.827 --> 18:13.961
<i>And eventually,
I became friends with six boys:</i>

18:13.997 --> 18:16.964
<i>Sebastian, Declan, Flibbit, Pipadoodle,</i>

18:17.000 --> 18:19.800
<i>Scudger and Nonsenseword.</i>

18:19.836 --> 18:21.302
<i>And together, we traveled...</i>

18:21.337 --> 18:23.305
<i>Okay, before you say
another name of anything,</i>

18:23.306 --> 18:24.739
<i>I'm going to have to stop you.</i>

18:24.774 --> 18:26.841
- Yes?
- I'm only here for 40 minutes,

18:26.876 --> 18:28.976
and this is already a really long story

18:29.012 --> 18:30.778
with a lot of locations.

18:30.813 --> 18:34.015
And I don't feel like waiting to
see how it links up to my thing.

18:34.050 --> 18:35.650
The point of the story is,

18:35.685 --> 18:37.652
I know what it's like to feel alone

18:37.687 --> 18:40.154
and to have to pretend that
you're something you're not.

18:40.189 --> 18:44.825
Oh, and also, how my first kiss
was with a Nazi.

18:44.894 --> 18:47.495
See? We didn't need
to go back 1,000 years for that.

18:47.530 --> 18:49.263
And I don't pretend
I'm something I'm not.

18:49.299 --> 18:51.198
- I never said that.
- You just said it.

18:51.234 --> 18:52.967
I might have implied it.

18:53.002 --> 18:56.971
I suppose the question is why
you feel the need to do that.

18:57.006 --> 18:59.273
I... I don't know.

18:59.309 --> 19:02.443
I-I guess I hadn't realized it before.

19:02.478 --> 19:05.946
Maybe I'm worried
nobody's going to like me.

19:05.982 --> 19:08.482
Lois left a tissue in the dryer again.

19:08.518 --> 19:11.619
And these are my only pants.
How sad is that?

19:11.654 --> 19:14.922
When I was in the orphanage,
my Sunday shoes were potatoes.

19:14.957 --> 19:16.624
Well, you win that one.

19:16.659 --> 19:18.392
Look, I'm different, Dr. Pritchfield.

19:18.428 --> 19:20.061
I'm not like the other kids.

19:20.096 --> 19:22.330
I realized that
when you picked up that photo

19:22.365 --> 19:24.332
and decimated my entire life.

19:24.367 --> 19:26.567
Sorry. It's kind of
a parlor trick of mine.

19:26.602 --> 19:28.502
Have you ever thought
of just being yourself...

19:28.538 --> 19:31.539
Your real self...
And then see what happens?

19:31.574 --> 19:34.041
I... I don't think I know how.

19:34.077 --> 19:37.545
Everything about me is this
carefully-constructed persona

19:37.580 --> 19:39.447
designed to keep people at arm's length.

19:39.482 --> 19:41.649
This isn't even how I really talk.

19:41.684 --> 19:44.452
This is. This is how I talk.

19:44.487 --> 19:46.620
The accent is-is nothing more
than an affectation,

19:46.656 --> 19:49.056
a-a coat of armor
to get me through the day,

19:49.092 --> 19:51.659
just an image I cultivated
so I could feel special.

19:53.329 --> 19:56.130
Wow. What a relief.

19:56.165 --> 19:58.432
For once, to talk with my real voice

19:58.468 --> 20:00.000
without the... the burden

20:00.069 --> 20:01.902
of trying to sound like someone I'm not.

20:01.938 --> 20:04.538
- What do you mean by that?
- Well, this is my real voice.

20:04.574 --> 20:06.173
I don't hear a difference.

20:06.209 --> 20:08.490
How about now? You freakin' hear that?

20:08.511 --> 20:10.379
What about now?
Sound different? Giggity?

20:10.380 --> 20:11.612
What about now?

20:11.647 --> 20:13.327
Coming up now, this voice.

20:13.349 --> 20:14.515
Ahoy. It's me.

20:14.550 --> 20:16.550
And I'm a gay alien.

20:16.586 --> 20:19.520
I'm sorry. That just all sounds
like the same person.

20:24.961 --> 20:28.162
Well, Stewie, that was quite
a breakthrough we just had.

20:28.197 --> 20:29.830
How do you feel?

20:29.866 --> 20:33.100
I feel... relieved,

20:33.136 --> 20:34.802
like a weight has been lifted.

20:34.837 --> 20:36.637
I-I can finally be myself.

20:36.672 --> 20:38.672
I like this Stewie.

20:38.708 --> 20:40.775
I hope he stays for a while.

20:40.810 --> 20:44.245
I-I've never been vulnerable
to anyone before.

20:44.280 --> 20:46.881
You're the only one
who's ever met the real me.

20:46.916 --> 20:48.249
Well, I'm honored, and now,

20:48.284 --> 20:50.484
the rest of the world is going
to meet him.

20:50.520 --> 20:52.019
Yes. Yes, that's right.

20:52.054 --> 20:53.854
There's no going back now!

20:53.890 --> 20:56.056
I'm gonna talk to Tyler.
I'm gonna make friends.

20:56.092 --> 20:57.625
I'm gonna be a normal boy!

20:57.660 --> 20:59.360
Yes, you are.

20:59.395 --> 21:01.695
You're finally going
to be just like everybody else.

21:01.731 --> 21:05.199
Wha-what? Say-say that again.

21:05.234 --> 21:08.235
You're finally going
to be just like everybody else.

21:08.271 --> 21:10.871
But... But I don't...

21:10.907 --> 21:14.475
<i>I don't want to be like everybody else.</i>

21:14.510 --> 21:15.709
What do you mean?

21:15.745 --> 21:17.578
<i>I-I don't want to be like them.</i>

21:17.647 --> 21:20.815
Like any other zero in this
miserable town, like my family.

21:20.850 --> 21:23.851
I won't do it! I don't want
to be like any other person!

21:23.886 --> 21:25.052
Like you skiing in...

21:25.087 --> 21:26.654
Please don't pick up that photo.

21:26.689 --> 21:28.622
I want to remain
what I've always been...

21:28.658 --> 21:30.891
Superior, brilliant, special.

21:30.927 --> 21:32.726
Oh, God, what was I thinking?!

21:32.762 --> 21:35.196
I'm never going to lift the veil, ever.

21:35.231 --> 21:37.832
Nobody will ever know the real me.

21:37.867 --> 21:39.266
My accent's back, by the way.

21:39.302 --> 21:40.768
It was a very dramatic moment.

21:40.803 --> 21:42.803
If you say so.

21:42.839 --> 21:47.839
Now, let's talk about...

21:51.814 --> 21:55.082
Stewie, can you hand me
my heart medication, please?

21:55.117 --> 21:57.151
Of course!

21:57.186 --> 21:59.687
Except...

21:59.722 --> 22:02.256
<i>there's one person
that does know the real me,</i>

22:02.291 --> 22:04.358
and that's you.

22:04.393 --> 22:05.659
I don't understand.

22:05.695 --> 22:08.062
Why aren't you getting my medication?

22:08.097 --> 22:09.964
Mm, yeah.

22:09.999 --> 22:12.566
Listen, you're not gonna like this.

22:12.602 --> 22:14.602
Please, there's not much time.

22:14.637 --> 22:17.304
I'd like to, really I would,
and you were super helpful,

22:17.340 --> 22:20.307
and this... this kind of seems
like a crummy way to repay you,

22:20.343 --> 22:22.109
but... I'm not going to get it.

22:22.144 --> 22:24.278
Is this because I made you wait?

22:24.313 --> 22:26.680
It's because I can't have anyone
know the real me.

22:26.716 --> 22:28.382
And so, you must die.

22:28.417 --> 22:30.951
I guess that's what happens
in therapy, though.

22:30.987 --> 22:33.120
No, it's not!

22:33.155 --> 22:35.122
Ah, you're right.
That didn't make much sense.

22:35.157 --> 22:36.657
Not to sound ghoulish,

22:36.692 --> 22:39.293
but any indication how much
longer you're going to linger?

22:39.328 --> 22:40.594
I'm not sure.

22:40.630 --> 22:42.330
I think it's different for everyone.

22:42.331 --> 22:43.999
Yeah, because you're still
pretty chatty, is...

22:44.000 --> 22:45.132
is why I'm wondering.

22:45.167 --> 22:46.667
Don't do this, Stewie!

22:46.702 --> 22:48.302
It will stay with you forever.

22:48.337 --> 22:51.305
Mm, yeah, not worried.
For me, this is kind of no bigs.

22:51.340 --> 22:53.641
Barbara! Barbara!

22:53.676 --> 22:55.342
Oh, didn't you hear her go to lunch?

22:55.378 --> 22:57.678
It was like an orchestra
of squeaky chair springs

22:57.713 --> 23:00.080
and groaning floorboards
playing her out.

23:00.116 --> 23:03.017
So... this is how you leave me.

23:03.052 --> 23:04.752
Sowwy.

23:07.590 --> 23:10.724
"Eyes, look your last!

23:10.760 --> 23:14.128
"Arms, take your last embrace.

23:14.163 --> 23:16.230
"And lips, O, you,

23:16.265 --> 23:19.600
"the doors of breath,
seal with a righteous kiss,

23:19.635 --> 23:24.635
a dateless bargain to engrossing death!"

23:28.444 --> 23:30.144
<i>Macbeth?</i>

23:30.212 --> 23:32.012
<i>Romeo and Juliet.</i>

23:32.048 --> 23:34.615
But I wouldn't expect you to know,

23:34.650 --> 23:37.851
since you're not in any way...

23:39.288 --> 23:40.654
British.

23:46.362 --> 23:48.529
I feel less bad.

23:50.800 --> 23:53.801
You've reached the office
of Dr. Cecil Pritchfield.

23:53.836 --> 23:55.736
Please leave a mess-age.

23:55.771 --> 23:57.571
That one can't be right.

23:57.607 --> 24:00.441
It's Michael. I want a divorce.

24:00.476 --> 24:02.643
<i>This is not your day.</i>

24:12.588 --> 24:14.455
Hey, you okay?

24:14.490 --> 24:17.157
Yes, Rupert just had
a nightmare, that's all.

24:17.193 --> 24:19.860
Go sleep on the floor
where you belong, whore!

24:19.895 --> 24:22.129
- All right, well...
- Hey... hey, Bri?

24:22.164 --> 24:23.831
Yeah?

24:23.866 --> 24:27.001
Would you mind maybe
sleeping with me tonight?

24:27.036 --> 24:29.036
- Sure.
- Thanks.

24:31.107 --> 24:33.574
Good night, Stewie.

24:33.609 --> 24:35.576
'Night, Brian.

24:36.979 --> 24:38.912
- Brian?
- Yeah?

24:38.948 --> 24:41.548
I did something awful.

24:41.584 --> 24:43.884
Do you want to talk about it?

24:43.953 --> 24:45.919
No.

24:54.710 --> 24:57.710
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru